{#- modules/identity/agents-different.j2
    Two slides on why agent identity is not client identity.

    whats_different() — a build-time → run-time axis over three before/after
    pairs, one key press per pair.
      aauth.dev "What Changed": "Software used to know at build time what it
        would call and what it would need. Now agents decide in the moment,
        against services they've never seen — the old protocols assumed
        neither." / "Clients used to be written. Now agents assemble them at
        runtime." / "Agents pick their tool chain at runtime, one call at a
        time." / under "Scopes Don't Capture Intent": "A scope is standing
        permission, not per-call intent. `mail.read` looks the same whether the
        agent is summarizing or scraping — policy can't tell them apart."
      aauth.dev "Why AAuth?": "In OAuth and OIDC, a client has no independent
        identity — a client_id at Google is meaningless at GitHub." / "It gives
        every HTTP client its own cryptographic identity and carries identity
        claims and authorization claims in the same token. It coexists with
        OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect rather than replacing them."
      draft-hardt-oauth-aauth-protocol-11 §1.2 "Agents Are Different":
        "Traditional software knows at build time what services it will call
        and what permissions it needs." / "Registration, key provisioning, and
        scope configuration happen before the first request." / "They discover
        resources at runtime." §1.3: "none needs pre-registration, so the first
        API call is the registration."
      draft-hardt-aauth-r3-00 (Status: Exploratory Draft) §3: "Each vocabulary
        corresponds to an API description format (MCP, OpenAPI, gRPC, GraphQL,
        AsyncAPI, WSDL, OData)." aauth.dev Specs card: "Vocabulary-based
        authorization using formats agents already understand (MCP, OpenAPI,
        gRPC, GraphQL), plus per-call proposals for operations authorized one
        call at a time."

    tokens() — OAuth's access token fans out into AAuth's five, then the
    refresh token has no counterpart. Two key presses.
      aauth.dev "How AAuth Works" glossary: "agent_token establishes the agent's
        identity / person_token names the person the agent acts for, at one
        resource / resource_token describes the access needed / auth_token
        grants an agent access to a resource / session-token issued by a
        resource for its own consumption, opaque to the agent".
      draft-hardt-oauth-aauth-protocol-11 §3 token definitions; §7.9 "AAuth has
        no refresh token. When an auth token expires, the agent obtains a fresh
        resource token from the resource and submits it to the PS, the same flow
        as the initial authorization."; Appendix C.4.5 "Bearer tokens: OAuth
        access tokens are bearer credentials — anyone who holds the token can
        use it. AAuth binds every token to a signing key"; §6.3 the session
        token "MAY be an existing OAuth access token".
      OAuth 2.1 draft-ietf-oauth-v2-1-16 §1.4 "An access token is a string
        representing an authorization issued to the client."
      The ID-token → person_token likeness is the speaker's analogy; the draft
      never maps its tokens onto OAuth's. person_token is drawn dashed for that
      reason.

    Import WITH CONTEXT.
      {%- from "modules/identity/agents-different.j2" import whats_different, tokens with context %}
-#}

{%- from "modules/branding/" ~ branding ~ "/primitives.j2" import content_slide, slide_heading -%}

{%- macro whats_different() -%}
{%- call content_slide("What's different") %}
<style>
  .ad-axis { position: relative; height: 4px; border-radius: 2px; margin: 3rem 0 1.75rem;
             background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--slide-ink-3), var(--accent-primary)); }
  .ad-axis span { position: absolute; top: -1.75rem; font-size: .85rem; font-weight: 700;
                  letter-spacing: .2em; color: var(--slide-ink-3); }
  .ad-axis .r { right: 0; color: var(--accent-primary); }
  .ad-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1rem 1.5rem; }
  .ad-card { padding: 1.1rem 1.4rem; border-radius: 1rem; background: var(--slide-surface);
             border: 1.5px solid var(--slide-hairline); }
  .ad-card.on { border-color: var(--accent-primary);
                background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-primary) 8%, var(--slide-surface)); }
  .ad-card b { display: block; font-size: 1.7rem; font-weight: 800; line-height: 1.15; color: var(--slide-ink); }
  .ad-card small { display: block; margin-top: .4rem; font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.3; color: var(--slide-ink-3); }
  .ad-card code { font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace; font-size: .92em; }
</style>
{{ slide_heading("", "How Agents are different") }}
<div class="ad-axis"><span>BUILD TIME</span><span class="r">RUN TIME</span></div>
<div class="ad-grid">
  {#- Titles only; the supporting lines live in the header comment, not on the slide.
      "Per resource": strictly a client_id is issued by each authorization server;
      per resource is the audience-level reading (each service you call hands you
      its own). aauth.dev: "a client_id at Google is meaningless at GitHub". -#}
  {%- for pair in [
    ("r1", "It knew what it would call", "It decides in the moment"),
    ("r2", "A Client ID, issued per resource", "Its own cryptographic identity"),
    ("r3", "Scopes granted up front", "Intent approved per call"),
  ] %}
  <div class="ad-card step" data-step="{{ pair[0] }}"><b>{{ pair[1] }}</b></div>
  <div class="ad-card on step" data-step="{{ pair[0] }}"><b>{{ pair[2] }}</b></div>
  {%- endfor %}
</div>
{%- endcall %}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro tokens() -%}
{%- call content_slide("One token became five") %}
<style>
  .tk { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 20rem 7rem 1fr; grid-template-rows: auto auto;
        column-gap: 0; row-gap: 2.5rem; align-items: center; margin-top: 2rem; }
  .tk-card { display: inline-block; font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
             font-size: 1.45rem; font-weight: 600; padding: .6rem 1.2rem; border-radius: .75rem;
             color: var(--slide-ink); background: var(--slide-surface); border: 1.5px solid var(--slide-rule);
             white-space: nowrap; }
  .tk-big { font-size: 2rem; padding: .9rem 1.5rem; }
  .tk-new { border-color: var(--accent-primary);
            background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-primary) 8%, var(--slide-surface)); }
  .tk-analogy { border-style: dashed; border-color: var(--accent-secondary); }
  .tk-none { border-style: dashed; border-color: var(--slide-ink-3); color: var(--slide-ink-3); }
  .tk-sub { font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.3; color: var(--slide-ink-3); }
  .tk-left { text-align: center; }
  .tk-left .tk-card { display: block; } /* fill the column so the connectors meet the card edge */
  .tk-left .tk-sub { margin-top: .6rem; }
  .tk-stack { display: grid; row-gap: .9rem; }
  .tk-row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 16rem 1fr; column-gap: 1.25rem; align-items: center; }
  .tk-row .tk-card { justify-self: start; }
  .tk svg { align-self: stretch; width: 100%; height: 100%; overflow: visible; }
  .tk svg path { fill: none; stroke: var(--accent-primary); stroke-width: 2; vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke; }
  .tk-refresh { position: relative; }
  .tk-refresh .tk-card { transition: opacity 1.4s; }
  .tk-refresh:has(.tk-x.step-visible) .tk-card { opacity: .18; }
  /* .tk .tk-x outranks the .tk svg connector rules above. Centred with margins,
     not transform: the engine's fadeIn keyframes animate transform, so a
     transform here would make the cross slide in from the wrong place. */
  .tk .tk-x { position: absolute; left: 50%; top: 50%; width: 7rem; height: 7rem; margin: -3.5rem 0 0 -3.5rem;
              overflow: visible; }
  .tk .tk-x.step-visible { animation: tk-fade .4s ease-out; } /* fade in place; the engine's fadeIn also slides up */
  @keyframes tk-fade { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }
  .tk .tk-x path { fill: none; stroke: var(--accent-danger); stroke-width: 9; stroke-linecap: round; stroke-linejoin: round;
                   vector-effect: none; }
  @media print { .tk-refresh .tk-card { opacity: .18 !important; } }
</style>
{{ slide_heading("", "More tokens!") }}
<div class="tk">
  <div class="tk-left">
    <span class="tk-card tk-big">access token</span>
  </div>
  {#- Connectors: viewBox maps 0..100 onto the middle cell, which is as tall as
      the stack, so row i of five is centred at (2i+1)*10. Fans from the access
      card's centre (0,50); non-scaling-stroke keeps the lines crisp despite
      preserveAspectRatio="none". -#}
  <svg class="step" data-step="split" viewBox="0 0 100 100" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true">
    {%- for y in [10, 30, 50, 70, 90] %}
    <path d="M0,50 C50,50 50,{{ y }} 100,{{ y }}"/>
    {%- endfor %}
  </svg>
  <div class="tk-stack">
    {%- for name, sub, cls in [
      ("agent_token", "establishes the agent&rsquo;s identity", ""),
      ("person_token", "names the person the agent acts for, at one resource", " tk-analogy"),
      ("resource_token", "describes the access needed", ""),
      ("auth_token", "grants an agent access to a resource", ""),
      ("session token", "issued by the resource for its own use, opaque to the agent", ""),
    ] %}
    <div class="tk-row step" data-step="split"><span class="tk-card tk-new{{ cls }}">{{ name }}</span><span class="tk-sub">{{ sub }}</span></div>
    {%- endfor %}
  </div>

  {#- Press 2: the refresh token greys out under a red cross. §7.9: "AAuth has
      no refresh token." The grey-out keys on the step's own .step-visible, so
      it lands with the cross; print forces both, like every other step. -#}
  <div class="tk-left tk-refresh">
    <span class="tk-card">refresh token</span>
    {#- A scrawled X: two uneven curved strokes, not a glyph, so it reads as
        struck out by hand and centres exactly on the card. -#}
    <svg class="tk-x step" data-step="refresh" viewBox="0 0 100 100" role="img" aria-label="no refresh token in AAuth">
      <g transform="rotate(-7 50 50)">
        <path d="M16,20 C34,36 52,56 86,84"/>
        <path d="M82,14 C64,30 46,54 14,88"/>
      </g>
    </svg>
  </div>
  <div></div><div></div>
</div>
{%- endcall %}
{%- endmacro -%}
